<p id="S2.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(S)</annotation></semantics></math> is the surface area of <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is at least <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth then <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(S)</annotation></semantics></math> is the infimum of radii corresponding to the smaller principal curvature
and <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(S)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(S)=0</annotation></semantics></math> as soon as <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> has a sharp corner. As an example consider the family <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> of oblate spheroids with semi-axes <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq a\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq a\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Here we have <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(S_{a})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(S_{a})=1</annotation></semantics></math> for every value of <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> (see SI, Sec 3), so <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(S_{a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(S_{a})</annotation></semantics></math> only depends on the surface area which is a monotonic function of <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> as
<math id="S2.SS4.p2.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(S_{a})=\sqrt{e/(e+a\,\text{artanh}(e))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>artanh</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(S_{a})=\sqrt{e/(e+a\,\text{artanh}(e))}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=\sqrt{1-a^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=\sqrt{1-a^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{artanh}(e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>artanh</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{artanh}(e)</annotation></semantics></math> is the inverse hyperbolic tangent of the excentricity <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>.
The sphere has <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(S_{1})=1/\sqrt{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(S_{1})=1/\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and the circular disc has <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(S_{0})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(S_{0})=1</annotation></semantics></math> (see Figure <a href="#S2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 2.4. Soft, softer, softest ‣ 2. Soft tilings and soft cells ‣ Soft cells and the geometry of seashells" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (b1)) and the latter case seams to be the global supremum of softness for all compact bodies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Using the above concept, we can now further deform a soft tiling to maximize its softness. We found some rules (see Materials and Methods, subsection <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3. Achieving high softness values ‣ 5. Materials and Methods ‣ Soft cells and the geometry of seashells" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>) which resulted in relatively high softness values. We compute and give the softness for the cells in Figure <a href="#S2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 2.4. Soft, softer, softest ‣ 2. Soft tilings and soft cells ‣ Soft cells and the geometry of seashells" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (d1-d4,f1-f3) by using (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.4. Soft, softer, softest ‣ 2. Soft tilings and soft cells ‣ Soft cells and the geometry of seashells" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). It is not clear whether any of these cells have maximal softness but the soft monohedric cells in panels (d3,d4, f1,f2,f3) corresponding to the 5 Dirichlet-Voronoi cells on lattices appear to have softness close to the maximum if the lattice is fixed (see SI, section 4A for details on the computation). However, in case of (d3) and (d4), softness can be further increased by changing the lattice so that the distance between the two curved faces decreases.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2402.04190v1/fig_4.png" id="S2.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/173;" width="476" height="173" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4. </span>Genesis of soft 3D cells. First row, panels a,c,e,g: non-soft cells. Second row, panels b,d,f,h: soft cells, with softness value indicated below each cell. Insets in white circles show the dual of the vertex polyhedron with Hamiltonian circuit indicated in red. II. Individual panels: (a1) Half cylinder: non-soft, non-spacefilling cell. (b1) Cylinder. Maximal softness (<math id="S2.F4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=1</annotation></semantics></math>) if <math id="S2.F4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=0</annotation></semantics></math> (two-sided circular disc). (c1) Space-filling <math id="S2.F4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-cell, resembling the Nautilus chamber. (c2-c4) Monohedral cells of prismatic tilings. (c2) Goldberg tetrahedron. (c3-c4): Dirichlet-Voronoi lattice <math id="S2.F4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-cells. (c3) cube (c4) hexagonal prism. (d1-d4): Soft versions of (c1-c4) as monohedric soft <math id="S2.F4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-cells. (e1-e3) Dirichlet-Voronoi lattice non-<math id="S2.F4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> cells. (e1) Elongated dodecahedron. (e2): Truncated octahedron. (e3): Rombododecahedron.(f1-f3): Soft versions of (c1-c3) as monohedric, soft, non-<math id="S2.F4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-cells. (g1) Non-spacefilling non-soft cell: Icosahedron. (h1) Non-spacefilling soft cell: sphere.</figcaption>
</figure>
